{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Every language gives strings a large toolbox. The tools are easy to use and easy to misuse: a single call can hide a full pass over the string, and inside a loop that turns O(n) into O(n²). Here is what each everyday operation is called in the four languages, and what it costs. n is the length of the string."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Reading"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Length** — `s.length` · `len(s)` · `s.length()` · `s.size()` — **O(1)**: stored with the string.",
        "**Character at i** — `s[i]` · `s[i]` · `s.charAt(i)` · `s[i]` — **O(1)**.",
        "**Loop over characters** — `for (const ch of s)` · `for ch in s` · `for (char ch : s.toCharArray())` · `for (char ch : s)` — **O(n)**. (Java's `toCharArray()` makes a copy; a plain index loop with `charAt` doesn't.)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Making new strings"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "All of these return a **new** string (except in C++ where noted), so each one costs at least the length of what it produces."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Substring** — `s.slice(a, b)` · `s[a:b]` · `s.substring(a, b)` · `s.substr(a, len)` — **O(b - a)**: it copies those characters.",
        "**Concatenate** — `s + t` in all four — **O(n + m)**.",
        "**Upper / lower case** — `toUpperCase()` · `upper()` · `toUpperCase()` · `toupper(c)` per character — **O(n)**.",
        "**Trim spaces at the ends** — `trim()` · `strip()` · `trim()` · (no built-in in C++) — **O(n)**.",
        "**Replace** — `replaceAll(a, b)` · `replace(a, b)` · `replace(a, b)` · loop with `find` — **O(n)** for simple text.",
        "**Reverse** — `[...s].reverse().join(\"\")` · `s[::-1]` · `new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString()` · `reverse(s.begin(), s.end())` (in place) — **O(n)**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/46804804-96ec-4076-ba4c-9e800c96699f.svg",
      "alt": "The string string with indexes 0 to 5. The substring from index 1 up to but not including 4 is t, r, i, highlighted. JavaScript slice(1, 4), Python [1:4] and Java substring(1, 4) all give tri. C++ substr(1, 3) also gives tri because its second argument is a length, not an end index.",
