    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Searching"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Find a substring** — `s.indexOf(t)` · `s.find(t)` · `s.indexOf(t)` · `s.find(t)` — returns the first index or -1 (`std::string::npos` in C++). **O(n · m)** in the worst case for a pattern of length m; Pattern 8 shows how to do it in O(n + m).",
        "**Contains** — `s.includes(t)` · `t in s` · `s.contains(t)` · `s.find(t) != npos` — same cost as find.",
        "**Starts / ends with** — `startsWith(t)` · `startswith(t)` · `startsWith(t)` · `s.rfind(t, 0) == 0` — **O(m)**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Splitting and joining"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Split into words** — `s.split(\" \")` · `s.split()` · `s.split(\" \")` · read with a `stringstream` — **O(n)**, and it builds a list of new strings.",
        "**Join a list** — `parts.join(\" \")` · `\" \".join(parts)` · `String.join(\" \", parts)` · append in a loop — **O(total length)**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Equal?** — `s === t` · `s == t` · `s.equals(t)` · `s == t` — **O(n)**: characters are compared until one differs. In Java, `==` only checks whether two variables point at the *same object* — always use `equals`.",
        "**Which comes first?** — `s < t` · `s < t` · `s.compareTo(t) < 0` · `s < t` — **lexicographic** order, like a dictionary: compare character by character, and a prefix comes before the longer word (`\"app\" < \"apple\"`). Uppercase letters come before lowercase, because their codes are smaller.",
        "**Sort the characters** — `[...s].sort().join(\"\")` · `\"\".join(sorted(s))` · `Arrays.sort(chars)` · `sort(s.begin(), s.end())` — **O(n log n)**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Checking characters"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Letter or digit?** — `/[a-z0-9]/i.test(ch)` · `ch.isalnum()` · `Character.isLetterOrDigit(ch)` · `isalnum(ch)`.",
        "**Digit value** — `ch - '0'` in Java and C++, `Number(ch)` in JavaScript, `int(ch)` in Python.",
        "**Change case of one character** — `ch.toLowerCase()` · `ch.lower()` · `Character.toLowerCase(ch)` · `tolower(ch)`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Putting it together"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A typical small task uses several tools at once. This one tidies a sentence: split it into words on any run of spaces, make each word start with a capital and continue in lower case, and join them with single spaces."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function titleCase(s) {\n  const words = s.trim().split(/\\s+/);          // split on runs of whitespace: O(n)\n  const out = [];\n  for (const w of words) {\n    if (w === \"\") continue;                     // an all-space input gives one empty word\n    out.push(w[0].toUpperCase() + w.slice(1).toLowerCase());\n  }\n  return out.join(\" \");                         // one O(n) join at the end\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def title_case(s):\n    words = s.split()                     # no argument: split on runs of whitespace, O(n)\n    return \" \".join(w[0].upper() + w[1:].lower() for w in words)"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "String titleCase(String s) {\n    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n    for (String w : s.trim().split(\"\\\\s+\")) {    // split takes a regular expression\n        if (w.isEmpty()) continue;               // an all-space input gives one empty word\n        if (sb.length() > 0) sb.append(' ');\n        sb.append(Character.toUpperCase(w.charAt(0)));\n        sb.append(w.substring(1).toLowerCase());\n    }\n    return sb.toString();\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "string titleCase(const string& s) {\n    stringstream in(s);                          // >> reads one word at a time, skipping spaces\n    string word, out;\n    while (in >> word) {\n        if (!out.empty()) out += ' ';\n        out += (char)toupper(word[0]);\n        for (size_t i = 1; i < word.size(); i++) out += (char)tolower(word[i]);\n    }\n    return out;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(n).** Every character is read a constant number of times: once by the split, once by the case changes, once by the join. Notice that none of these tools was called *inside a loop over the whole string* — that's the habit that keeps string code linear."
    }
  ],
